NOTES = {
    "title": "This chapter of the book, SQLite Databases, shows how a Python program keeps data in a database file with the standard sqlite3 module: it creates tables, reads and changes rows, protects values with placeholders, groups changes into transactions, speeds up searches with indexes, links tables, and copies a database. The deck has twenty-five code examples that run on the chapter page, six true stories, and a plain explanation of every concept. Slide 2 lays out the five parts.",
    "route": "The route has five parts, and each row gives the slides of the part, how many code examples it holds and what you will run. Part 1 opens and defines databases and tables, part 2 reads and changes rows safely, part 3 covers transactions, indexes and schema changes, part 4 links and copies tables, and part 5 gives two practice programs. The last row covers the tools table, the list of what differs from the book today, the summary, the practice questions and the resources.",
    "questions": "Two questions frame the chapter, and the answers come back at the end. The first asks which statements change the database, which only read it, and what decides whether a change is kept, undone or refused; slide 50 answers it in a table. The second asks which printed outputs, programs and statements of the book differ in current Python and SQLite; slide 51 answers it from the chapter research. Keep both in mind while you read the code slides, and note which slide each answer builds on.",
    "divider1": "Part 1 is about databases, tables and connections. A database is a file or a piece of memory, and a connection is the handle a program holds on it. The four cards are the ideas of the part: a connection, a STRICT table whose column types are rules, the storage class of a stored value, and the hidden number of each row. Slides 5 to 14 cover them, and part 2 then reads and changes the rows of such a table.",
    "divider2": "Part 2 is about reading and changing rows, and about keeping values out of the SQL text. The cards name its ideas: WHERE and ORDER BY choose and sort rows, NULL means that no value is known, a placeholder is a slot filled with data and never with code, and SQL injection is text that rewrites the query it was pasted into. Slides 15 to 25 cover them. Part 3 then groups several changes into one transaction.",
    "divider3": "Part 3 is about transactions, indexes and changes to the schema. The cards name its ideas: a transaction is a group of changes that stand or fall together, a rollback undoes every change since BEGIN, an index is a sorted list that speeds some searches, and VACUUM rebuilds the file without its empty space. Slides 26 to 37 cover them. Part 4 then links several tables and copies whole databases.",
    "divider4": "Part 4 is about several tables, copies and backups. The cards name its ideas: a foreign key is a column that must name an existing row, a join lays rows of two tables side by side, backup() copies a whole database even from memory, and iterdump() writes a database out as SQL text. Slides 38 to 45 cover them. Part 5 then combines the tools in two practice programs.",
    "divider5": "Part 5 gives the two practice programs of the book, run on small stand-in data typed into the code, because the book's own database file is not reachable offline. The cat checker finds impossible dates and missing shots, and the meal program tells which meal the kitchen can cook. The cards add GROUP BY, which gathers rows that share a value, and HAVING, which keeps only the groups that qualify. Slides 46 to 49 cover them.",
    "A1": "A database stores rows in tables with typed columns, while a spreadsheet leaves the types to you. SQLite is a library and a single file with no server to start, and the sqlite3 module in the standard library lets Python open it. A program connects to a database, which is a file or memory, uses the connection to send statements, and closes it at the end. Slide 7 shows these three steps in code, and slide 6 tells why SQLite was built as one file.",
    "A2": "A table has a name and a list of columns, and each column has a declared type such as TEXT, INTEGER or REAL. CREATE TABLE defines the table. In an ordinary table the declared type is only a preference called type affinity, so SQLite converts a value when it can and stores the rest. A STRICT table makes the type a rule and refuses a wrong value. The storage class tells what was really stored. Slides 10 and 11 show these ideas in code.",
    "A3": "A foreign key is a column whose value must name an existing row of another table, and SQLite enforces it only after the switch is turned on with a PRAGMA. A join lays rows of two tables side by side where the linking values match: INNER JOIN keeps only the rows with a match, and LEFT JOIN keeps every row of the left table. Each row also has a rowid, and an INTEGER PRIMARY KEY is that same number. Slides 41 and 42 show keys and joins in code.",
    "A4": "Create, read, update and delete, called CRUD, are the four things a program does to rows. INSERT adds a row, and executemany adds many with one statement by running it once for each tuple. SELECT reads rows and changes nothing: fetchall returns every result row as a list of tuples, and fetchone returns the first row or None. The same table appears in the code on slides 13 and 17. Updating and deleting follow in the next part of the chapter.",
    "A5": "A query narrows what it returns with a few clauses. WHERE keeps only the rows that satisfy a condition, ORDER BY sorts them, and LIMIT stops after a number of rows. LIKE compares text with the wildcard % and ignores case for the 26 ASCII letters, while GLOB uses * and ? and is case sensitive. A placeholder, written ? or :name, is a slot whose value travels beside the statement. Slides 17 and 19 show the clauses and the two comparisons in code.",
    "A6": "UPDATE changes columns of every row that the WHERE clause selects, and rowcount says how many rows that was. DELETE removes the selected rows, and without WHERE it removes all of them. RETURNING hands back columns of the changed or deleted rows and needs SQLite 3.35. executemany runs one statement for each tuple of a list. SQL injection is text pasted into a statement that rewrites the query, and a placeholder prevents it. Slides 22 to 24 show these in code.",
    "A7": "A transaction groups changes so that they are all kept or all undone, which is the atomic part of ACID: atomic, consistent, isolated and durable. BEGIN opens a transaction, commit makes its changes permanent, and rollback undoes every change since BEGIN. In autocommit mode every statement is saved at once unless a transaction is open. The contextlib module offers helpers for blocks that must clean up, such as closing(). Slides 28 to 32 show these in code.",
    "A8": "An index is a sorted list on a column, built with CREATE INDEX, that lets some searches skip most rows; DROP INDEX removes it. EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN shows how SQLite would run a query without running it, and the plan says SEARCH when an index is used and SCAN when every row is read. VACUUM rebuilds the file without its empty space. In journal mode SQLite writes each change to a journal before changing the file, and the write-ahead log is a separate log. Slides 36 and 37 show them.",
    "A9": "ALTER TABLE changes the design of an existing table: it can add, rename or drop a column. DROP TABLE removes a table and all its rows, and IF EXISTS makes dropping a missing table harmless. A backup is a copy kept apart from the database, and a database can be copied in three ways: backup() copies every page, even from memory, iterdump() yields SQL statements that rebuild it, and a closed file can be copied with shutil. Slides 34 and 43 to 45 show them in code.",
    "A10": "The last group of concepts gathers the tools around the practice programs. executescript runs a whole text of statements at once, shutil copies and moves files, pprint prints a structure with one item per line, and an in-memory database lives in RAM and vanishes when the program ends, so backup() can copy it to a file first. The cat vaccination checker and the meal ingredients database are the two practice programs of the book. Slides 48 and 49 run them on small stand-in data.",
    "connect_close": "The first step of every database program is a connection. connect() opens a database, and ':memory:' keeps it in RAM only. in_transaction is False until a statement starts a transaction. execute() sends one statement, and fetchone() returns the first result row as a tuple, here (42,). close() ends the connection, and after it every statement is refused with an error, which the program catches so that it can go on. Slide 32 shows another way to close a connection.",
    "create_table": "CREATE TABLE defines a table with its columns and their types, and the keyword STRICT after the column list makes each type a rule. NOT NULL refuses a cat without a name. sqlite_schema is the table where SQLite keeps its own list of tables, so one query lists them, and PRAGMA table_info lists the columns of one table: position, name, type, the NOT NULL flag, a default and the primary-key flag. The next slide shows what STRICT refuses.",
    "strict_types": "Two tables hold the same column, one STRICT and one ordinary. The text Hello goes into both: the STRICT table refuses it with an error, and the ordinary table stores it, because its declared type is only a preference called type affinity. The text 42, however, is converted to an integer in the STRICT table, and typeof() names the storage class of each stored value. Slide 12 tells when SQLite added STRICT, and slide 51 lists the type words it accepts.",
    "insert_read": "INSERT adds a row, and the values can be typed into the statement. executemany runs one statement once for each tuple of a list, so two more rows go in at once. A SELECT then reads the rows back: fetchall returns every row as a list of tuples, and fetchone returns the first. The hidden column rowid numbers the rows from 1, and the connection counts the rows changed so far, here 3. The next slide shows when a rowid can be reused.",
    "rowid_pk": "A column declared INTEGER PRIMARY KEY is another name for the rowid, so rowid and id show the same number and SQLite numbers new rows itself. After row 3 is deleted, a new row takes number 3 again, because without AUTOINCREMENT SQLite may reuse the largest number that was freed. The last inserted id is therefore 3, not 4. Slide 51 notes that the book says the rowid does not change, and that VACUUM may renumber rows.",
    "select_filter": "A table of three cats holds a fur colour and a weight in kilograms. The WHERE clause keeps only the black cats, ORDER BY kg DESC puts the heaviest first, and the ? takes its value from the tuple. The result is Zophie and Toby with their weights. In the second query LIMIT 2 stops after two rows, here the two heaviest cats of all three, Miguel and Zophie. The clauses combine in this order: WHERE, then ORDER BY, then LIMIT.",
    "like_glob": "A small helper runs a statement and returns its rows, so that each line stays short. LIKE and GLOB each answer 1 for a match and 0 for none. LIKE matches in any case and uses % as the wildcard; GLOB is case sensitive and uses * and ?. The first test writes the pattern in lower case: LIKE matches and GLOB does not. The second writes it with the capital Z, and both match. LIKE ignores case only for the 26 ASCII letters, so for é and É it finds no match. Slide 51 adds that double quotes around text are a legacy habit.",
    "null_logic": "NULL means that no value is known; it is not an empty text and not zero. In the table, Mandy's fur is NULL. count(*) counts all three rows, while count(fur) skips the NULL and counts two. Comparing with NULL is never true, not even fur != 'black', so those queries return nothing for Mandy. Only IS NULL finds her. This is why a test for a missing value must be written IS NULL, and why a filter on one value can hide the rows with no value.",
    "update_delete": "Here each statement is saved at once, because isolation_level=None turns on autocommit. One UPDATE changes every row that the WHERE clause selects, and rowcount says how many rows that was, here 2. DELETE removes rows, and RETURNING hands back a column of the deleted rows, so the program sees that Jacob was removed; RETURNING needs SQLite 3.35 or newer. A final SELECT shows the two cats that remain with their new weights.",
    "placeholders": "A placeholder is a slot in the statement that is filled with a value when the statement runs. A ? takes the next value of a tuple, by position, and a :name takes the value of that key in a dictionary, by name. Both queries return the same two cats. The value travels beside the statement and never inside it, and that is what makes a placeholder safe. The next slide shows what goes wrong when a value is pasted into the SQL instead.",
    "injection": "A web form asks a visitor for a cat's name and searches for it. The visitor types a clever text. Pasted into the statement, it closes the quote and adds OR '1'='1', which is always true, so the search returns both cats. Bound with a placeholder, the same text is only a name; no cat has it, so no row is found. This is the idea of the comic on slide 25. A program should never build SQL by pasting user text into it.",
    "commit_rollback": "In autocommit mode every statement is saved at once, unless the program opens a transaction itself with BEGIN. Inside the transaction the new row is visible to the program, and in_transaction is True. A rollback throws the change away, so the count falls back to 0. A second transaction ends with a commit, which makes the change permanent and ends the transaction: the count is 1 and in_transaction is False. Slide 51 notes that Python 3.12 added an autocommit attribute as the recommended switch.",
    "atomic_stmt": "A table has a column that refuses NULL. One statement inserts three rows, and the third breaks the rule. The whole statement is refused, and afterwards the table holds 0 rows: rows 1 and 2 were undone too, because a statement is atomic, all of it or none of it. A later good insert of two rows then leaves 2 rows. A failed statement therefore leaves no trace, even without an explicit transaction.",
    "context_manager": "A with block on the connection is a transaction. Leaving the block without an error commits the insert, so the count is 1. An error inside the block rolls back every change made in it, the good row 2 too, so after the second block the table still holds 1 row. The error is caught afterwards so that the program goes on. The with statement therefore gives commit on success and rollback on error; the next slide shows what it does not do.",
    "with_closing": "A with block on the connection ends a transaction, but it does not close the connection, which still answers afterwards: here it returns (42,). contextlib.closing() is the block that closes the connection at its end, and then the next statement is refused with an error. A program that must release the database file should therefore use closing() as well as the with block. Slide 7 showed a plain close().",
    "alter_drop": "A table with one row shows what the changes do to it. ALTER TABLE adds a new column and renames another, and the old row stays, with NULL in its new column. DROP COLUMN needs SQLite 3.35. DROP TABLE IF EXISTS removes the table, and IF EXISTS makes dropping a missing table harmless. After DROP TABLE nothing is left: the count of entries in sqlite_schema is 0.",
    "index_plan": "An index on the fur column is a sorted list that points to the rows. EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN says how SQLite would run a query, and the program keeps only the first word of the answer: SEARCH means that an index is used, and SCAN means that every row is read. Only the query on the indexed column searches. A search on the name and a search for the middle of a text with LIKE both scan, because the default index does not serve them. Slide 51 repeats this point.",
    "file_vacuum": "A database is a file. Before connect() no file exists in the new folder, and afterwards it does. Two thousand rows of 100 characters fill it. DELETE leaves the file as big as before, because the space is kept for new rows. VACUUM rebuilds the file without that space, so the file shrinks. The program prints only yes or no for the size, so that the output does not depend on the SQLite version or the machine.",
    "foreign_key": "SQLite does not enforce foreign keys until a program switches them on with PRAGMA foreign_keys, once per connection, and the first line shows that it is 0 by default. The column meal_id of the ingredients must name a row of the meals table. Meal 1 exists, so flour is accepted; meal 99 does not exist, so salt is refused with a foreign key error. Slide 51 notes that the book's own foreign key cannot be enforced as written.",
    "inner_left_join": "Two tables hold the cats and the vaccinations they received, and the number in cat_id is the id of the cat. Mandy has no vaccination yet. INNER JOIN drops Mandy, who has no match, and returns three rows. LEFT JOIN keeps every row of the left table, so Mandy appears with None for the vaccine. The choice between the two joins decides whether a row without a partner is dropped or kept.",
    "backup_copy": "A memory database with two cats vanishes when the program ends, so it is worth copying. backup() copies every page of the source into a destination database, which here is in a file, and the copy then holds 2 rows. The copy is a snapshot: a later change to the source does not reach it, so the copy still holds 2 rows after the source has changed. A backup protects against loss, not against a wrong change that was copied.",
    "iterdump": "A database with one table and one row is written out as text. iterdump() yields the SQL statements that rebuild the database one line at a time: BEGIN TRANSACTION, CREATE TABLE, INSERT and COMMIT. executescript() runs the whole text at once in a new database, and that database holds the same row. A dump is plain text, so it can be stored, compared and replayed. Slide 51 notes that in Python 3.13 iterdump fails on the book's own database because of a foreign key mismatch.",
    "shutil_copy": "A database file is an ordinary file, so the shutil module can copy it, and the copy opens like any database: it holds the row that was kept. The program commits and closes first, because a copy of a file that is being written can be broken. Three ways of copying a database are now known: backup() for a live one, iterdump() for a text, and shutil for a closed file. The practice programs on the next slides use none of them.",
    "cat_checker": "The first practice program of the book checks vaccination records. The book's database is not reachable offline, so two cats and their shots are typed in, with the same kinds of columns. Toby's FVRCP shot is dated before his birth, which is a data error to find. Dates written year-month-day compare correctly as text. The second query finds the cats that miss a kind of vaccine, here Zophie and Toby, because each cat has had only one kind of shot.",
    "meal_ingredients": "The second practice program asks which meal the kitchen can cook. One table holds a meal and one ingredient per row, and a tuple called have holds what the kitchen has. GROUP BY gathers the rows of each meal, and HAVING keeps only the meals for which no ingredient is missing from have, so only the Omelette can be cooked. The tuple is bound to three placeholders, which keeps the values out of the SQL text.",
    "tools": "The table answers the first question of slide 3. SELECT only reads and has nothing to keep. INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE write rows, and a change is kept at commit, or at once in autocommit. CREATE, ALTER and DROP write the schema at once unless a transaction is open. A rule that breaks refuses the statement and leaves no trace. A rollback undoes everything since BEGIN. PRAGMA reads or sets a switch, mostly per connection, and VACUUM rewrites the file. The last column names the code slide of each row.",
    "current": "The table answers the second question of slide 3, from the chapter research of 12 October 2026 with Python 3.14.6 and SQLite 3.53.1. It lists what the book says, what is true today and the example that shows it, for instance that a foreign key to the rowid cannot be enforced, that STRICT takes six type words, that Python 3.12 added the autocommit attribute, and that a memory database is not much faster, because the commit decides the speed. Run what the book printed, and then check what changed.",
    "summary": "The summary keeps five points to remember, and each can be traced to a slide. A database is a file or memory and a connection is the handle on it. A STRICT table makes column types a rule, and NULL is not a value. Values are bound with placeholders and never pasted into SQL. A transaction is all or nothing: commit keeps and rollback undoes. A database is copied with backup, iterdump or a closed file, and the copy should be checked. The chart counts the code examples in each part.",
    "practice": "The practice questions can be answered in a few lines, and most can be checked by running code on the chapter page. The first asks which rows WHERE fur != 'black' returns when one fur is NULL, and why; slide 20 shows the answer. The second asks what changes when two thousand inserts are wrapped in one transaction. The third asks why the foreign key example inserts the meal first. The fourth extends the cat checker to list cats born after their first shot. Predict first, then run.",
    "resources": "The resources are the book chapter, the documentation of the sqlite3 module, the pages of sqlite.org on data types, STRICT tables, foreign keys, transactions, write-ahead logging, query plans and appropriate uses, the OWASP page on preventing SQL injection, xkcd 327, the IBM history of the relational database, a Wikipedia page on Codd, and the book Practical SQL. Every link is live; pick one and read it before the next class.",
}

STORY_NOTES = {
    "hipp": "D. Richard Hipp designed SQLite in the spring of 2000 for a damage-control program of the United States Navy. The photograph shows Hipp, and the picture credit below it names the source. A database that is one file needs no server to start and no user accounts, and that choice shapes everything in this chapter: a connection is only a call that opens a file. The link leads to the history page of SQLite.",
    "codd": "Edgar F. Codd described the relational model in a paper of June 1970. His idea, data kept in tables that are linked by values, is what a SQLite file still holds: the cats table and the vaccinations table joined by a number on slide 42 are that idea in a few lines. The photograph shows Codd, and the picture credit below it names the source. The link leads to the IBM history page.",
    "python25": "Python 2.5, released on 19 September 2006, brought the sqlite3 package into the standard library, so that a program can keep data in a database without installing anything. Which SQLite version a program gets still depends on the Python build, which is why slide 51 names the versions of the research. The picture shows Guido van Rossum, who led the Python developers, with its credit below. The link leads to the release notes.",
    "strict": "SQLite 3.37.0, released on 27 November 2021, added STRICT tables. Before it, a column's declared type was only a preference, and text could land in an INTEGER column, as slide 11 shows for the ordinary table. The picture is the banner of the SQLite site, with its credit below it. The chapter builds every table with the keyword STRICT. The link leads to the release log on sqlite.org.",
    "bobby": "Randall Munroe published xkcd comic 327, called Exploits of a Mom, on 10 October 2007. A boy's name closes a statement and drops the table of student records, and the mother hopes the school has learned to sanitize its database inputs. It is the best-known picture of SQL injection, which slide 24 shows in code. The photograph shows the author, with its credit below. The link leads to the comic.",
    "acid": "Theo Haerder and Andreas Reuter named the promises of a transaction ACID in 1983: atomicity, consistency, isolation and durability. They built on Jim Gray, who is shown here, and who had named three of them in 1981, but not isolation. Slide 29 shows the atomic part in code: a failed statement leaves no trace. SQLite is tested with simulated power loss to keep these promises. The link leads to the Wikipedia page on ACID.",
}

